1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,797
The steering wheel of a Formula One

2
00:00:02,797 --> 00:00:05,435
car has more than twenty buttons,

3
00:00:05,435 --> 00:00:06,954
dials and switches,

4
00:00:06,954 --> 00:00:09,672
a screen in the middle and paddles

5
00:00:09,672 --> 00:00:10,472
behind it.

6
00:00:10,652 --> 00:00:13,263
And the driver uses them at three

7
00:00:13,263 --> 00:00:15,478
hundred kilometres per hour,

8
00:00:15,478 --> 00:00:16,665
with one thumb,

9
00:00:16,665 --> 00:00:17,931
between corners.

10
00:00:18,111 --> 00:00:20,108
It is not a steering wheel.

11
00:00:20,288 --> 00:00:22,502
It is the control room of the

12
00:00:22,502 --> 00:00:22,807
car.

13
00:00:22,987 --> 00:00:26,023
Today, ten parts of the F1 cockpit,

14
00:00:26,023 --> 00:00:28,104
and why each one exists,

15
00:00:28,104 --> 00:00:30,186
starting with the wheel.

16
00:00:30,536 --> 00:00:33,615
The cockpit of a Formula One car

17
00:00:33,615 --> 00:00:36,213
is a tiny carbon fibre tub.

18
00:00:36,393 --> 00:00:38,494
The driver lies almost flat,

19
00:00:38,494 --> 00:00:41,121
with the feet higher than the hips,

20
00:00:41,121 --> 00:00:43,448
wrapped in a seat shaped to his

21
00:00:43,448 --> 00:00:43,823
body.

22
00:00:44,003 --> 00:00:46,277
There is no room for anything that

23
00:00:46,277 --> 00:00:47,614
does not have a job.

24
00:00:47,794 --> 00:00:50,854
Every button, every strap and every piece

25
00:00:50,854 --> 00:00:53,615
of clothing in there was designed for

26
00:00:53,615 --> 00:00:56,452
speed, or written into the rules after

27
00:00:56,452 --> 00:00:57,720
someone got hurt.

28
00:00:58,071 --> 00:01:01,184
As always, every fact here comes from

29
00:01:01,184 --> 00:01:02,446
a named source,

30
00:01:02,446 --> 00:01:06,401
the FIA, Formula One and motorsport references,

31
00:01:06,401 --> 00:01:08,926
all listed in the description.

32
00:01:09,106 --> 00:01:11,510
Let's take the wheel off and look

33
00:01:11,510 --> 00:01:12,020
closer.

34
00:01:12,370 --> 00:01:15,251
Why does an F1 steering wheel have

35
00:01:15,251 --> 00:01:16,607
so many buttons?

36
00:01:16,787 --> 00:01:20,070
Because the driver is also the engineer

37
00:01:20,070 --> 00:01:20,827
on board.

38
00:01:21,007 --> 00:01:23,883
A modern Formula One car is a

39
00:01:23,883 --> 00:01:26,659
hybrid, with a turbo engine,

40
00:01:26,659 --> 00:01:29,634
electric motors and a battery.

41
00:01:29,814 --> 00:01:32,278
The driver can change how the engine

42
00:01:32,278 --> 00:01:35,016
delivers power, how the battery is used,

43
00:01:35,016 --> 00:01:37,411
how the brakes are balanced and how

44
00:01:37,411 --> 00:01:39,122
the differential behaves,

45
00:01:39,122 --> 00:01:40,286
corner by corner.

46
00:01:40,466 --> 00:01:42,646
All of that is done from the

47
00:01:42,646 --> 00:01:43,113
wheel.

48
00:01:43,463 --> 00:01:45,123
Some buttons are simple.

49
00:01:45,303 --> 00:01:46,879
One for the radio,

50
00:01:46,879 --> 00:01:49,944
one for the pit lane speed limiter,

51
00:01:49,944 --> 00:01:52,133
one to confirm a message,

52
00:01:52,133 --> 00:01:53,534
one for neutral.

53
00:01:53,714 --> 00:01:56,604
Others change whole maps of settings with

54
00:01:56,604 --> 00:01:57,662
a single click.

55
00:01:57,842 --> 00:02:00,362
Every team designs its own wheel,

56
00:02:00,362 --> 00:02:03,036
and each driver can ask for buttons

57
00:02:03,036 --> 00:02:04,564
in different places,

58
00:02:04,564 --> 00:02:06,015
for his own thumbs.

59
00:02:06,195 --> 00:02:09,272
Each wheel is a handmade carbon fibre

60
00:02:09,272 --> 00:02:12,931
computer, reported to cost tens of thousands

61
00:02:12,931 --> 00:02:13,846
of dollars.

62
00:02:14,196 --> 00:02:17,801
Drivers spend hours in the simulator learning

63
00:02:17,801 --> 00:02:21,406
the wheel, until the movements are automatic.

64
00:02:21,586 --> 00:02:22,732
During the race,

65
00:02:22,732 --> 00:02:25,097
the engineer on the radio may say

66
00:02:25,097 --> 00:02:27,032
a code, like a mode number,

67
00:02:27,032 --> 00:02:29,970
and the driver changes it without looking

68
00:02:29,970 --> 00:02:30,328
down.

69
00:02:30,508 --> 00:02:33,146
The most important skill is not knowing

70
00:02:33,146 --> 00:02:34,026
every button.

71
00:02:34,206 --> 00:02:36,694
It is knowing which one to press,

72
00:02:36,694 --> 00:02:39,484
at three hundred kilometres per hour,

73
00:02:39,484 --> 00:02:41,218
without losing a tenth.

74
00:02:41,568 --> 00:02:44,598
Why do F1 drivers keep turning small

75
00:02:44,598 --> 00:02:47,292
dials on the wheel during a lap?

76
00:02:47,472 --> 00:02:49,619
One of the most used is brake

77
00:02:49,619 --> 00:02:50,212
balance.

78
00:02:50,392 --> 00:02:52,840
It decides how much braking goes to

79
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:55,079
the front wheels and how much to

80
00:02:55,079 --> 00:02:55,709
the rear.

81
00:02:55,889 --> 00:02:58,592
As the fuel burns and the tyres

82
00:02:58,592 --> 00:03:00,510
wear, the car changes.

83
00:03:00,690 --> 00:03:03,285
A balance that was perfect on lap

84
00:03:03,285 --> 00:03:05,801
one can lock the front wheels on

85
00:03:05,801 --> 00:03:06,588
lap forty.

86
00:03:06,768 --> 00:03:08,836
So the driver adjusts it,

87
00:03:08,836 --> 00:03:11,319
sometimes for a single corner.

88
00:03:11,669 --> 00:03:14,494
Another dial controls the differential,

89
00:03:14,494 --> 00:03:16,739
the part that lets the two rear

90
00:03:16,739 --> 00:03:19,347
wheels turn at different speeds in a

91
00:03:19,347 --> 00:03:19,854
corner.

92
00:03:20,034 --> 00:03:22,806
More locked helps traction out of slow

93
00:03:22,806 --> 00:03:23,389
corners.

94
00:03:23,569 --> 00:03:25,752
More open helps the car turn in.

95
00:03:25,932 --> 00:03:28,565
Drivers change it between corners,

96
00:03:28,565 --> 00:03:31,121
and on some cars the setting even

97
00:03:31,121 --> 00:03:34,839
changes automatically at different points of the

98
00:03:34,839 --> 00:03:37,240
corner, entry, middle and exit.

99
00:03:37,590 --> 00:03:40,428
Other dials manage the hybrid system,

100
00:03:40,428 --> 00:03:43,496
how much energy the electric motor gives

101
00:03:43,496 --> 00:03:45,951
and how much it takes back under

102
00:03:45,951 --> 00:03:46,564
braking.

103
00:03:46,744 --> 00:03:49,070
Watch an onboard camera closely,

104
00:03:49,070 --> 00:03:51,687
and you will see the driver's thumbs

105
00:03:51,687 --> 00:03:54,012
moving on almost every straight.

106
00:03:54,192 --> 00:03:55,086
They are not playing.

107
00:03:55,266 --> 00:03:57,486
They are tuning a race car while

108
00:03:57,486 --> 00:03:58,180
racing it.

109
00:03:58,530 --> 00:04:01,084
Why are there paddles behind an F1

110
00:04:01,084 --> 00:04:02,210
steering wheel?

111
00:04:02,390 --> 00:04:04,294
The top ones change gear.

112
00:04:04,474 --> 00:04:05,881
Right paddle up,

113
00:04:05,881 --> 00:04:07,376
left paddle down,

114
00:04:07,376 --> 00:04:09,838
through eight forward gears.

115
00:04:10,018 --> 00:04:13,006
A modern F1 gearbox changes gear in

116
00:04:13,006 --> 00:04:15,568
a few thousandths of a second,

117
00:04:15,568 --> 00:04:18,215
so fast that there is almost no

118
00:04:18,215 --> 00:04:19,666
gap in the power.

119
00:04:19,846 --> 00:04:22,239
The driver never takes his hands off

120
00:04:22,239 --> 00:04:24,300
the wheel, and there is no gear

121
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:26,162
lever in the cockpit at all.

122
00:04:26,512 --> 00:04:28,551
The lower paddles are the clutch,

123
00:04:28,551 --> 00:04:30,529
and there are often two of them.

124
00:04:30,709 --> 00:04:33,165
The clutch is only used to start,

125
00:04:33,165 --> 00:04:35,473
at the race start and leaving a

126
00:04:35,473 --> 00:04:36,142
pit stop.

127
00:04:36,322 --> 00:04:38,311
Before the lights go out,

128
00:04:38,311 --> 00:04:41,015
the driver holds one paddle at the

129
00:04:41,015 --> 00:04:44,037
perfect bite point and the other fully

130
00:04:44,037 --> 00:04:44,275
in.

131
00:04:44,455 --> 00:04:46,004
When the lights go out,

132
00:04:46,004 --> 00:04:48,159
he lets go of the second paddle,

133
00:04:48,159 --> 00:04:50,448
and the car launches from the bite

134
00:04:50,448 --> 00:04:50,852
point.

135
00:04:51,202 --> 00:04:53,627
Getting that bite point right is one

136
00:04:53,627 --> 00:04:55,985
of the hardest skills in the sport.

137
00:04:56,165 --> 00:04:58,197
Too much and the wheels spin.

138
00:04:58,377 --> 00:05:01,001
Too little and the engine bogs down.

139
00:05:01,181 --> 00:05:04,360
The rules banned electronic launch control,

140
00:05:04,360 --> 00:05:07,021
so the start depends on the driver's

141
00:05:07,021 --> 00:05:07,613
fingers.

142
00:05:07,793 --> 00:05:10,000
Races have been won and lost in

143
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:11,567
the first two seconds,

144
00:05:11,567 --> 00:05:13,633
on a paddle behind the wheel.

145
00:05:13,983 --> 00:05:16,537
Why do F1 drivers pull the steering

146
00:05:16,537 --> 00:05:18,580
wheel off when they get out?

147
00:05:18,760 --> 00:05:21,593
Because the cockpit is so tight that

148
00:05:21,593 --> 00:05:24,268
a driver cannot climb out with the

149
00:05:24,268 --> 00:05:25,448
wheel in place.

150
00:05:25,628 --> 00:05:27,595
Behind the wheel there is a quick

151
00:05:27,595 --> 00:05:28,310
release hub.

152
00:05:28,490 --> 00:05:30,403
The driver pulls a collar,

153
00:05:30,403 --> 00:05:33,052
and the wheel slides off the column.

154
00:05:33,232 --> 00:05:35,602
The rules require a driver to be

155
00:05:35,602 --> 00:05:37,527
able to get out of the car

156
00:05:37,527 --> 00:05:38,712
in five seconds.

157
00:05:39,062 --> 00:05:42,050
The five second test is done with

158
00:05:42,050 --> 00:05:43,590
full racing gear,

159
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:45,854
helmet, gloves and belts.

160
00:05:46,034 --> 00:05:48,936
It exists for fire and for crashes.

161
00:05:49,116 --> 00:05:51,509
A driver who is trapped in a

162
00:05:51,509 --> 00:05:53,559
burning car has seconds,

163
00:05:53,559 --> 00:05:54,585
not minutes.

164
00:05:54,765 --> 00:05:56,229
The wheel comes off,

165
00:05:56,229 --> 00:05:58,866
the six point harness opens with one

166
00:05:58,866 --> 00:06:00,330
twist of the buckle,

167
00:06:00,330 --> 00:06:02,747
and the driver jumps out over the

168
00:06:02,747 --> 00:06:04,285
sides of the cockpit.

169
00:06:04,635 --> 00:06:07,111
When drivers stop their car on the

170
00:06:07,111 --> 00:06:09,588
track, you will often see them put

171
00:06:09,588 --> 00:06:12,356
the wheel back on before walking away.

172
00:06:12,536 --> 00:06:15,092
That is because marshals may need to

173
00:06:15,092 --> 00:06:17,152
push or steer the car off the

174
00:06:17,152 --> 00:06:19,709
track, and the car cannot be steered

175
00:06:19,709 --> 00:06:20,988
without the wheel.

176
00:06:21,168 --> 00:06:22,321
A small habit,

177
00:06:22,321 --> 00:06:24,546
but it is in the rules too.

178
00:06:24,896 --> 00:06:27,490
Why does a row of coloured lights

179
00:06:27,490 --> 00:06:29,533
flash at the top of the F1

180
00:06:29,533 --> 00:06:30,713
steering wheel?

181
00:06:30,893 --> 00:06:32,831
These are the shift lights.

182
00:06:33,011 --> 00:06:34,954
As the engine revs climb,

183
00:06:34,954 --> 00:06:37,440
the lights fill up from one side

184
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:38,450
to the other,

185
00:06:38,450 --> 00:06:40,314
and when they all flash,

186
00:06:40,314 --> 00:06:42,956
it is the perfect moment to change

187
00:06:42,956 --> 00:06:43,344
gear.

188
00:06:43,524 --> 00:06:45,554
The driver does not look at a

189
00:06:45,554 --> 00:06:46,044
number.

190
00:06:46,224 --> 00:06:48,393
His eyes stay on the track and

191
00:06:48,393 --> 00:06:51,070
he sees the colours in his peripheral

192
00:06:51,070 --> 00:06:51,576
vision.

193
00:06:51,926 --> 00:06:54,156
The car also has lights for the

194
00:06:54,156 --> 00:06:55,235
race officials.

195
00:06:55,415 --> 00:06:58,335
Small panels in the cockpit light up

196
00:06:58,335 --> 00:06:59,795
yellow, blue, red,

197
00:06:59,795 --> 00:07:02,391
or the colour of the safety car,

198
00:07:02,391 --> 00:07:04,986
at the same moment the flags are

199
00:07:04,986 --> 00:07:07,501
shown on the side of the track.

200
00:07:07,681 --> 00:07:10,248
A driver might miss a flag waved

201
00:07:10,248 --> 00:07:12,655
by a marshal in a fast corner,

202
00:07:12,655 --> 00:07:15,382
so the flag comes into the cockpit

203
00:07:15,382 --> 00:07:15,703
too.

204
00:07:16,053 --> 00:07:18,601
And on the screen in the middle

205
00:07:18,601 --> 00:07:19,669
of the wheel,

206
00:07:19,669 --> 00:07:22,053
the driver can see lap times,

207
00:07:22,053 --> 00:07:23,861
the gap to other cars,

208
00:07:23,861 --> 00:07:26,409
tyre temperatures and warnings.

209
00:07:26,589 --> 00:07:29,266
Teams customise the screen pages,

210
00:07:29,266 --> 00:07:32,025
so each driver sees what he wants,

211
00:07:32,025 --> 00:07:33,485
where he wants it.

212
00:07:33,665 --> 00:07:36,484
All this information has to be readable

213
00:07:36,484 --> 00:07:37,496
in one glance,

214
00:07:37,496 --> 00:07:39,303
at the end of a straight,

215
00:07:39,303 --> 00:07:41,038
with a vibrating helmet.

216
00:07:41,388 --> 00:07:43,607
Why is there a drink button on

217
00:07:43,607 --> 00:07:45,161
an F1 steering wheel?

218
00:07:45,341 --> 00:07:48,279
Because drivers lose a lot of water.

219
00:07:48,459 --> 00:07:51,004
In a hot race like Singapore,

220
00:07:51,004 --> 00:07:54,428
with high humidity and almost two hours

221
00:07:54,428 --> 00:07:57,588
of driving, a driver can lose around

222
00:07:57,588 --> 00:08:00,309
three kilograms of body weight,

223
00:08:00,309 --> 00:08:01,450
mostly sweat.

224
00:08:01,630 --> 00:08:03,871
A bottle sits inside the car,

225
00:08:03,871 --> 00:08:06,344
and a tube runs into the helmet.

226
00:08:06,524 --> 00:08:07,674
Press the button,

227
00:08:07,674 --> 00:08:09,366
and a pump sends a sip of

228
00:08:09,366 --> 00:08:11,261
drink to the driver's mouth.

229
00:08:11,611 --> 00:08:13,747
The system does not always work.

230
00:08:13,927 --> 00:08:15,757
Sometimes the pump fails,

231
00:08:15,757 --> 00:08:18,318
and the driver has nothing to drink

232
00:08:18,318 --> 00:08:19,709
for the whole race.

233
00:08:19,889 --> 00:08:21,978
And the drink is often hot,

234
00:08:21,978 --> 00:08:24,763
because the bottle sits in a cockpit

235
00:08:24,763 --> 00:08:27,548
that can be well above forty degrees

236
00:08:27,548 --> 00:08:28,631
in a hot race.

237
00:08:28,811 --> 00:08:31,719
Drivers describe it as drinking hot tea

238
00:08:31,719 --> 00:08:33,583
while running a marathon.

239
00:08:33,933 --> 00:08:37,590
Dehydration is dangerous in a race car.

240
00:08:37,770 --> 00:08:40,443
A small loss of water can slow

241
00:08:40,443 --> 00:08:42,225
reactions and focus,

242
00:08:42,225 --> 00:08:44,809
and in Formula One a tenth of

243
00:08:44,809 --> 00:08:48,195
a second of reaction is several metres

244
00:08:48,195 --> 00:08:48,997
of track.

245
00:08:49,177 --> 00:08:51,852
So the teams weigh drivers before and

246
00:08:51,852 --> 00:08:54,599
after races, and plan exactly how much

247
00:08:54,599 --> 00:08:57,130
they should drink before the start.

248
00:08:57,310 --> 00:09:00,528
Even water has a strategy in Formula

249
00:09:00,528 --> 00:09:00,886
One.

250
00:09:01,236 --> 00:09:03,349
Why does an F1 seat come out

251
00:09:03,349 --> 00:09:05,764
of the car with the driver still

252
00:09:05,764 --> 00:09:06,217
in it?

253
00:09:06,397 --> 00:09:09,187
Each seat is a hard carbon fibre

254
00:09:09,187 --> 00:09:12,327
shell, moulded to the exact shape of

255
00:09:12,327 --> 00:09:13,897
one driver's body.

256
00:09:14,077 --> 00:09:16,484
And it is designed to be lifted

257
00:09:16,484 --> 00:09:17,803
out in one piece.

258
00:09:17,983 --> 00:09:19,930
If a driver is injured in a

259
00:09:19,930 --> 00:09:22,525
crash, the medical team can take him

260
00:09:22,525 --> 00:09:24,904
out still strapped into the seat,

261
00:09:24,904 --> 00:09:27,283
without bending his back or neck.

262
00:09:27,633 --> 00:09:30,381
Back and neck injuries are the biggest

263
00:09:30,381 --> 00:09:32,189
fear after a heavy crash,

264
00:09:32,189 --> 00:09:34,721
because a wrong movement can turn a

265
00:09:34,721 --> 00:09:36,457
fracture into paralysis.

266
00:09:36,637 --> 00:09:38,452
With the extraction seat,

267
00:09:38,452 --> 00:09:41,211
the seat itself becomes the stretcher.

268
00:09:41,391 --> 00:09:44,993
Medical teams practice this procedure at every

269
00:09:44,993 --> 00:09:46,481
race, on real cars,

270
00:09:46,481 --> 00:09:48,674
so they can do it in minutes

271
00:09:48,674 --> 00:09:49,692
on any track.

272
00:09:50,042 --> 00:09:52,696
Making the seat is a ritual for

273
00:09:52,696 --> 00:09:54,152
every new driver.

274
00:09:54,332 --> 00:09:56,196
He sits in a bag of liquid

275
00:09:56,196 --> 00:09:57,630
foam inside the car,

276
00:09:57,630 --> 00:09:59,279
in his racing position,

277
00:09:59,279 --> 00:10:02,076
until the foam hardens around his body.

278
00:10:02,256 --> 00:10:04,694
That shape becomes the mould for the

279
00:10:04,694 --> 00:10:05,506
carbon seat.

280
00:10:05,686 --> 00:10:08,084
It fits so closely that the driver

281
00:10:08,084 --> 00:10:10,694
feels every bump of the track through

282
00:10:10,694 --> 00:10:13,091
it, which is exactly what he needs

283
00:10:13,091 --> 00:10:14,220
to feel the car.

284
00:10:14,570 --> 00:10:17,060
Why do race car drivers wear this

285
00:10:17,060 --> 00:10:19,551
carbon collar on their shoulders?

286
00:10:19,731 --> 00:10:21,471
It is the HANS device,

287
00:10:21,471 --> 00:10:24,003
short for head and neck support.

288
00:10:24,183 --> 00:10:26,469
Two straps connect the back of the

289
00:10:26,469 --> 00:10:27,881
helmet to the collar,

290
00:10:27,881 --> 00:10:30,301
which sits under the shoulder belts.

291
00:10:30,481 --> 00:10:31,931
In a sudden stop,

292
00:10:31,931 --> 00:10:34,490
the body is held by the belts,

293
00:10:34,490 --> 00:10:37,389
but the head keeps moving forward.

294
00:10:37,569 --> 00:10:39,535
HANS holds the head back with the

295
00:10:39,535 --> 00:10:39,833
body.

296
00:10:40,183 --> 00:10:42,640
Without it, the neck can be stretched

297
00:10:42,640 --> 00:10:44,832
so violently that the base of the

298
00:10:44,832 --> 00:10:45,895
skull fractures.

299
00:10:46,075 --> 00:10:49,072
It is called a basilar skull fracture,

300
00:10:49,072 --> 00:10:51,439
and it killed several drivers.

301
00:10:51,619 --> 00:10:54,772
The device was developed in the nineteen

302
00:10:54,772 --> 00:10:56,901
eighties by Robert Hubbard,

303
00:10:56,901 --> 00:10:58,872
an engineering professor,

304
00:10:58,872 --> 00:11:01,631
and his brother in law Jim Downing,

305
00:11:01,631 --> 00:11:02,892
a racing driver.

306
00:11:03,072 --> 00:11:05,301
For years few drivers used it.

307
00:11:05,651 --> 00:11:07,788
Then, in two thousand one,

308
00:11:07,788 --> 00:11:10,911
NASCAR legend Dale Earnhardt died of a

309
00:11:10,911 --> 00:11:14,363
basilar skull fracture at the Daytona five

310
00:11:14,363 --> 00:11:15,020
hundred.

311
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:18,293
After that, series around the world made

312
00:11:18,293 --> 00:11:19,298
it mandatory.

313
00:11:19,478 --> 00:11:22,879
Formula One made HANS compulsory from two

314
00:11:22,879 --> 00:11:24,122
thousand three.

315
00:11:24,302 --> 00:11:26,828
Today no top racing driver starts a

316
00:11:26,828 --> 00:11:27,983
race without it.

317
00:11:28,163 --> 00:11:30,575
A collar on the shoulders is the

318
00:11:30,575 --> 00:11:33,968
reason many drivers walked away from crashes.

319
00:11:34,318 --> 00:11:37,348
Why don't F1 race suits catch fire?

320
00:11:37,528 --> 00:11:39,916
They are made of layers of a

321
00:11:39,916 --> 00:11:42,815
fire resistant fibre called Nomex,

322
00:11:42,815 --> 00:11:45,800
the same family of material used by

323
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:46,909
firefighters.

324
00:11:47,089 --> 00:11:50,072
The suits are tested to protect against

325
00:11:50,072 --> 00:11:52,596
direct flame for several seconds,

326
00:11:52,596 --> 00:11:54,891
enough for a driver to escape.

327
00:11:55,071 --> 00:11:59,689
Underneath, drivers wear fireproof underwear,

328
00:11:59,689 --> 00:12:02,768
socks, a balaclava and gloves.

329
00:12:02,948 --> 00:12:05,793
Every layer is certified by the FIA.

330
00:12:06,143 --> 00:12:07,607
In twenty twenty,

331
00:12:07,607 --> 00:12:09,845
at the Bahrain Grand Prix,

332
00:12:09,845 --> 00:12:13,117
Romain Grosjean's car hit a barrier at

333
00:12:13,117 --> 00:12:16,733
more than two hundred kilometres per hour,

334
00:12:16,733 --> 00:12:20,005
split in two and exploded into flames.

335
00:12:20,185 --> 00:12:22,644
He was inside the fire for almost

336
00:12:22,644 --> 00:12:23,761
thirty seconds.

337
00:12:23,941 --> 00:12:26,043
He climbed out with burns on his

338
00:12:26,043 --> 00:12:26,437
hands.

339
00:12:26,617 --> 00:12:28,410
His suit, his helmet,

340
00:12:28,410 --> 00:12:30,970
the halo over his head and the

341
00:12:30,970 --> 00:12:34,043
fire marshals all did their job that

342
00:12:34,043 --> 00:12:34,384
day.

343
00:12:34,734 --> 00:12:37,644
Every race suit also has two handles

344
00:12:37,644 --> 00:12:39,018
on the shoulders.

345
00:12:39,198 --> 00:12:41,668
They are there so that marshals and

346
00:12:41,668 --> 00:12:43,926
doctors can pull a driver out of

347
00:12:43,926 --> 00:12:46,396
the car, even if he is unconscious.

348
00:12:46,576 --> 00:12:49,096
The suits are surprisingly light,

349
00:12:49,096 --> 00:12:50,470
around a kilogram,

350
00:12:50,470 --> 00:12:53,372
because a heavier suit means more heat

351
00:12:53,372 --> 00:12:54,517
and more sweat.

352
00:12:54,697 --> 00:12:57,777
The race suit is a compromise between

353
00:12:57,777 --> 00:12:59,608
a furnace and a sauna.

354
00:12:59,958 --> 00:13:02,825
Why do F1 drivers' gloves have a

355
00:13:02,825 --> 00:13:04,079
sensor inside?

356
00:13:04,259 --> 00:13:06,550
Since twenty eighteen,

357
00:13:06,550 --> 00:13:10,924
Formula One drivers wear biometric gloves.

358
00:13:11,104 --> 00:13:12,417
A small sensor,

359
00:13:12,417 --> 00:13:14,168
sewn into the glove,

360
00:13:14,168 --> 00:13:17,846
measures the driver's pulse and the oxygen

361
00:13:17,846 --> 00:13:18,984
in his blood,

362
00:13:18,984 --> 00:13:21,436
and sends it by radio to the

363
00:13:21,436 --> 00:13:22,574
medical team.

364
00:13:22,754 --> 00:13:24,195
After a big crash,

365
00:13:24,195 --> 00:13:26,597
before anyone reaches the car,

366
00:13:26,597 --> 00:13:29,799
the doctors already know if the driver's

367
00:13:29,799 --> 00:13:31,160
heart is working.

368
00:13:31,510 --> 00:13:33,605
It is a reaction to a simple

369
00:13:33,605 --> 00:13:34,203
problem.

370
00:13:34,383 --> 00:13:35,399
After a crash,

371
00:13:35,399 --> 00:13:37,504
the car may be upside down or

372
00:13:37,504 --> 00:13:38,447
in a barrier,

373
00:13:38,447 --> 00:13:40,624
and the driver may not be able

374
00:13:40,624 --> 00:13:42,220
to speak on the radio.

375
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:43,965
With the glove sensor,

376
00:13:43,965 --> 00:13:46,597
the medical car can decide how urgent

377
00:13:46,597 --> 00:13:48,801
it is while it is still driving

378
00:13:48,801 --> 00:13:49,726
to the scene.

379
00:13:49,906 --> 00:13:52,208
And the data are also used to

380
00:13:52,208 --> 00:13:55,543
study how drivers' bodies react to crashes

381
00:13:55,543 --> 00:13:57,766
and to the stress of racing.

382
00:13:58,116 --> 00:14:00,597
The gloves still have to be perfect

383
00:14:00,597 --> 00:14:01,448
for driving.

384
00:14:01,628 --> 00:14:02,659
They are thin,

385
00:14:02,659 --> 00:14:05,165
so the driver can feel the buttons

386
00:14:05,165 --> 00:14:06,122
on the wheel,

387
00:14:06,122 --> 00:14:08,701
and fireproof, like the rest of his

388
00:14:08,701 --> 00:14:09,291
clothes.

389
00:14:09,471 --> 00:14:11,926
The sensor is the size of a

390
00:14:11,926 --> 00:14:14,382
coin and almost weightless.

391
00:14:14,562 --> 00:14:15,771
In Formula One,

392
00:14:15,771 --> 00:14:18,673
even the gloves are connected to the

393
00:14:18,673 --> 00:14:20,367
internet of the race.

394
00:14:20,717 --> 00:14:22,238
So here are the ten.

395
00:14:22,418 --> 00:14:25,146
A wheel full of buttons because the

396
00:14:25,146 --> 00:14:27,329
driver is also the engineer.

397
00:14:27,509 --> 00:14:30,551
Dials for brake balance and the differential.

398
00:14:30,731 --> 00:14:33,424
Paddles for gears and two clutches for

399
00:14:33,424 --> 00:14:34,132
the start.

400
00:14:34,312 --> 00:14:37,412
A wheel that comes off in seconds.

401
00:14:37,592 --> 00:14:39,777
Lights that say when to shift,

402
00:14:39,777 --> 00:14:40,869
a drink button,

403
00:14:40,869 --> 00:14:43,199
a seat that becomes a stretcher,

404
00:14:43,199 --> 00:14:44,801
a collar for the neck,

405
00:14:44,801 --> 00:14:46,695
a suit that does not burn,

406
00:14:46,695 --> 00:14:49,098
and gloves that check your heart.

407
00:14:49,448 --> 00:14:52,013
In the cockpit of a Formula One

408
00:14:52,013 --> 00:14:54,579
car, every object has two jobs,

409
00:14:54,579 --> 00:14:57,227
to make the driver faster and to

410
00:14:57,227 --> 00:14:58,469
bring him home.

411
